    82	If `G=0`, the whole central line lies in the singular locus, so the
    83	hypersurface fails `R1` and is not normal.  If `G!=0`, only the finite
    84	zero scheme of `G` can be singular on the central line.  Together with a
    85	smooth generic fibre, the hypersurface is regular in codimension one; being
    86	Cartier, it is Cohen--Macaulay and hence `S2`.  Thus the local total surface
    87	is normal.  The gate below uses only the necessary direction `normal =>
    88	G!=0`, but the converse identifies the exact local normality test under
    89	generic smoothness.
