    91	## 2. Hessian derivation
    92	
    93	For a ternary cubic `U`, put
    94	
    95	```text
    96	H(U)=-1/2 det(d^2 U).                                    (2.1)
    97	```
    98	
    99	Fisher's Hessian-pencil identity implies
   100	
   101	```text
   102	[mu] H(U+mu H(U))   = 3 c4(U) U,
   103	[mu^2]H(U+mu H(U)) = 6 c6(U) U-3 c4(U)H(U).             (2.2)
   104	```
   105	
   106	Because the `x^3` coefficient of (0.1) is a unit, equations (2.2) recover
   107	`c4,c6` from the single `x^3` coefficient of the pencil Hessian.  Applying
   108	this to the universal truncated form
   109	
   110	```text
